@prefix hanoi: <http://example.org/orexis/hanoi#> .
@prefix : <http://example.org/orexis/hanoi#> .
@prefix orexis: <http://example.org/orexis#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix owl:  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix xsd:  <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix sh:   <http://www.w3.org/ns/shacl#> .

<http://example.org/orexis/hanoi> a owl:Ontology ;
    rdfs:label "Orexis hanoi" ;
    rdfs:comment """Tower of Hanoi, as a domain plug-in (#257). The claim under test is AGENTS.md's own — the domain is a plug-in, plant/water language is the example — and this package is the proof at its strongest: a classical planning domain that ships an ontology and ONE move action and not one line of Python. The goal is a world-ratified desire met by absence (a disk off the target peg); legality lives in the move's precondition; the state is graph triples an effect really retracts — the first retraction that deletes rather than upserts; and the optimal solution is nobody's algorithm, it is the cheapest achiever under orexis:costs.""" .

#################  The board  #################
#  THE PEGS ARE T-BOX INDIVIDUALS because they are the puzzle's fixtures: every Tower of
#  Hanoi ever posed has exactly these three, so a world that owned its own pegs would be
#  restating an invariant. (They first moved here for a second reason — six GROUND actions
#  had to name them, and code may not name a world instance — and that reason retired with
#  the grid when the sovereign ruled one Move and an effect could see its row.) The DISKS are
#  the world's: a two-disk world and a three-disk world differ only there.

:Peg a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Peg" ;
    rdfs:comment "One of the three rods — a T-Box individual each, because the pegs are the puzzle's invariant and a world authors only its disks." .
:PegA a :Peg ; rdfs:label "peg A" ; rdfs:comment "Where every puzzle starts." .
:PegB a :Peg ; rdfs:label "peg B" ; rdfs:comment "The spare." .
:PegC a :Peg ; rdfs:label "peg C" ; rdfs:comment "Where every goal points." .

:Disk a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Disk" ;
    rdfs:comment "A world's individuals, sized. How many there are is the world's whole authorship of an instance of this puzzle." .

:size a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain :Disk ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "size" ;
    rdfs:comment "Smaller number, smaller disk. Legality is one comparison: a move onto a peg holding any smaller disk is no move at all — with an ordered start that is exactly the classical rule, said without naming tops." .

:on a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain :Disk ;
    rdfs:label "on" ;
    rdfs:comment "What a disk rests on — another disk, or a peg. RUNTIME STATE, never authored in a world: it lives in the state graph, effects retract and add it, and the chain (hanoi:on)+ down to a peg is what 'on peg P' means everywhere in this package's queries." .

#  WHAT A MOVE IS FILLED WITH. Two parameters, named for what they are: the disk that moves
#  and the peg it moves onto. They are this package's words — the kernel holds a row to them
#  and reads neither — and one spelling serves the precondition's `?disk`, the effect rules'
#  `$disk` and the predicate a planned step is written under.

:disk a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Action ;
    rdfs:label "disk" ;
    rdfs:comment "The disk a move moves. Declared by hanoi:Move as orexis:takes, bound by its precondition to whatever is on top of a stack." .

:onto a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Action ;
    rdfs:label "onto" ;
    rdfs:comment "The peg a move moves its disk onto. Bound to a peg holding no smaller disk; the effect walks to the top of whatever rests there." .

#  WHAT SOLVING MEANS, AND HOW FAR IT IS — the puzzle's, not a world's. A world asserts the
#  want and points here; both used to sit inline in `world/hanoi/desire.ttl`, and the courier
#  package's ontology says why they moved. The shape is compiled by the kernel; the estimate
#  is run by it against whichever world is being judged, which the rule never names (#666).

:solved a sh:NodeShape ;
    rdfs:label "every disk's chain bottoms out on peg C" ;
    rdfs:comment "What solving MEANS, said positively: on every disk IN PLAY — one resting on something — following `hanoi:on` one or more times reaches peg C. Targeted on the subjects of `on` rather than on the class, because a world declares its disks and a puzzle may pose fewer: the two-disk puzzle is posed on a world that declares three, and a disk resting on nothing is not astray, it is not in play. The kernel compiles this into the select whose rows are the disks astray (#497). The object of a want's orexis:metWhen." ;
    sh:targetSubjectsOf :on ;
    sh:property [ sh:path [ sh:oneOrMorePath :on ] ; sh:hasValue :PegC ] .

:disksAstray sh:select """
        SELECT (COUNT(DISTINCT ?d) AS ?estimate) WHERE {
             ?d hanoi:on ?rest . ?d (hanoi:on)+ ?p .
            ?p a hanoi:Peg .
            FILTER(?p != hanoi:PegC)
        }""" ;
    rdfs:label "disks not yet home" ;
    rdfs:comment "How far the puzzle still is, in moves: every disk astray must move at least once more, so the count never overstates what is left — which is all orexis:estimates asks, and the promise the search ends on. A WEAK floor, deliberately: the optimal path moves disks away from C to free the ones beneath, and a count cannot see that. Measured: 56 forks to 50 on three disks. The object of a want's orexis:estimates." .
